AISE-0.3 — Mathematical and Experimental Specification for Artificial Inherited Sensory Evolution

Inheriting Perceptual Structure Without Inheriting Its Interpretation

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

We present AISE-0.3, a corrected mathematical and experimental specification for the intergenerational transmission of perceptual structure in evolutionary robotics. The framework defines a perceptual genome as an ordered sequence of gammatone filter triples, a leaky-integrator envelope detector with a corrected time constant, a recurrent controller whose parameters are reset between generations, and two dimension-preserving ablation operators for estimating marginal filter contribution. Two distillation operators are separated: supervised distillation, which optimizes present utility, and perceptual distillation, which preserves spectral diversity via a max-min dispersion objective with a known 2-approximation guarantee. The organizational control P\text{-}S permutes the sequential assignment of filter triples while preserving all marginal distributions. The experimental design corrects for generational data leakage, equalizes active filter capacity across groups, and pre-registers a primary comparison with an appropriately powered statistical plan.

---

1. Introduction and Positioning

1.1 Established background

Lamarckian inheritance of controller parameters accelerates evolution and raises newborn fitness, and this benefit has been demonstrated to persist under nonstationary environmental conditions, with the Lamarckian system achieving up to 33\% higher fitness than a Darwinian baseline in challenging dynamic terrains. Sensor–controller co-evolution is feasible: sensor sensitivities and temporal resolutions can be jointly optimized with controllers. Evolutionary optimization of receptive fields has been demonstrated on robotic arms and in active-vision systems. Evolutionary optimization of gammatone filter banks exists in the speech-processing literature. Knowledge distillation between robot generations exists for controllers (policies), not for perceptual structures; multi-teacher policy distillation has been shown to produce controllers that generalize zero-shot to new morphologies.

Baldwinian evolution, in which learning does not alter the genotype but shapes the selection pressure on the genotype, is a related but distinct mechanism that has been studied in neural network evolution. LEAF, in its original usage, refers to an evolutionary AutoML framework that optimizes hyperparameters and network architectures; it is not a perceptual-distillation method, and AISE should not be conflated with it. The positioning of AISE relative to Baldwinian meta-learning and evolutionary AutoML is as follows: AISE transmits perceptual structure, not policy parameters, not hyperparameters, and not learning algorithms. The genotype encodes a filter bank; the phenotype is the lifetime-trained controller. This is a narrower and more specific claim than either Baldwinian meta-learning or evolutionary AutoML.

1.2 Hypothesis to be tested

H1 (Main hypothesis). A filter-bank structure distilled from one generation, transmitted without controller parameters, and re-adapted by the next generation reduces the number of learning episodes required to acquire a new predictive relationship in a semantically shifted environment, compared to: (a) no inheritance, (b) random selection of the same size, and (c) a permuted organization.

1.3 What is not claimed

We do not claim that the inherited structure carries meaning; only capacity to detect is transmitted. We do not claim that reinterpretation, if observed, implies consciousness or subjective experience. No systematic review has been completed, and the novelty claim is provisional.

