2. Mathematical Framework

2.1 Signal model

Let x_t \in \mathbb{R}, t \in \{1,\dots,T\}, be a discrete-time signal sampled at rate f_s. The environment \mathcal{E} generates signals by superposition:

x_t = \sum_{k=1}^{K} a_{k,t} \sin\!\left(2\pi f_k t / f_s + \phi_k\right) + \epsilon_t,
\qquad
\epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2),

with event-dependent amplitude envelopes a_{k,t} = a_k \, g(t - t_{\text{evt}}; \sigma_k), where g is a smooth pulse and t_{\text{evt}} is the event onset. Events belong to \mathcal{Y} = \{A, B, C, \varnothing\}.

A fixed shared component at f_\star is present in A and B in the base environment, and in C in the semantic environment. The spectral composition of each environment is specified precisely in Section 3.1.

2.2 Perceptual genome

Definition 1 (Genome). A perceptual genome is an ordered sequence of filter triples

G = \big( (f_i, b_i, \tau_i) \big)_{i=1}^{m},
\qquad
f_i, b_i, \tau_i \in \mathbb{R}_{>0},

where f_i is the center frequency, b_i is the bandwidth parameter, and \tau_i is the temporal-integration constant. The ordering i=1,\dots,m is part of the genome and defines its organization.

2.3 Filter bank and envelope extraction

Gammatone impulse response of order n:

h_{f_i,b_i}(t) = t^{\,n-1} e^{-2\pi b_i t} \cos(2\pi f_i t + \varphi),
\qquad t \ge 0.

The bandwidth parameter is

b_i = 1.019 \, \operatorname{ERB}(f_i),
\qquad
\operatorname{ERB}(f) = 24.7 + 0.108 f,

where the factor 1.019 accounts for the difference between the gammatone bandwidth and the ERB scale. The temporal-integration constant is initialized to span several carrier periods:

\tau_i = \frac{k}{f_i},
\qquad k \ge 3.

This ensures that the integrator cutoff is well below the carrier frequency f_i, so that ripple at 2f_i is attenuated by at least a factor of k/(2\pi). Filtered output:

u_{i,t} = (h_{f_i,b_i} * x)_t.

Envelope extraction. First-order leaky integrator:

z_{i,t} = \alpha_i \, z_{i,t-1} + (1-\alpha_i)\, |u_{i,t}|,
\qquad
\alpha_i = \exp\!\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_i}\right),

with \Delta t = 1/f_s and z_{i,0}=0. The steady-state gain is unity: for constant input |u_{i,t}| = c, z_{i,\infty} = c. The effective integration window is \tau_{\text{eff}} = \tau_i.

Spectral separability check. Before the full experiment, verify that the ERB-overlap between filters in [20,50] Hz and the filter at f_\star = 100 Hz is below a threshold \rho_{\max} (e.g., \rho_{\max} = 0.3). If separability fails, adjust f_\star or the filter count m until the oracle condition holds (see Section 4.4).

2.4 Controller and lifetime learning

Recurrent controller:

h_t = \tanh(W_{hh} h_{t-1} + W_{zh} z_t + b_h),
\qquad
\hat{y}_t = \operatorname{softmax}(W_{hy} h_t + b_y).

Lifetime learning minimizes cross-entropy over \mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}:

\theta^\star = \arg\min_\theta \; \mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim \mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}} \big[ \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{C}_\theta(\Phi_G(x)), y) \big].

Reset rule. For all perceptual-inheritance groups, \theta is drawn from a fixed random initialization at every generation. For the Lamarckian control group L\text{-}\theta, \theta is inherited from the parental controller while G is held fixed. Only G is inherited in the perceptual groups.

2.5 Marginal contribution — two ablations

Definition 2 (Zero-ablation, fixed dimension). For trained \theta^\star, set z_{i,t} \leftarrow 0 for all t, keep all other filters unchanged:

\Delta_i^{\text{zero}} = J\!\left(\theta^\star, \Phi_G^{(i \to 0)}\right) - J\!\left(\theta^\star, \Phi_G\right).

Input dimension is preserved, so W_{zh} is unchanged. \Delta_i^{\text{zero}} > 0 indicates that removing filter i worsens performance.

Definition 3 (Retrained ablation). Remove filter i; train fresh \theta_{-i} on G \setminus i:

\Delta_i^{\text{retrain}} = \min_{\theta} J\!\left(\theta, \Phi_{G \setminus i}\right) - J\!\left(\theta^\star, \Phi_G\right).

\Delta_i^{\text{zero}} is primary; \Delta_i^{\text{retrain}} is secondary. Both computed on \mathcal{D}_{\text{sel}}, disjoint from train, validation, and test sets.

2.6 Two distillation operators

Definition 4 (Supervised distillation). Given threshold \varepsilon > 0:

G^{\text{sup}} = \big( (f_i,b_i,\tau_i) \big)_{i \,:\, \Delta_i^{\text{zero}} \,>\, \varepsilon}.

Definition 5 (Perceptual distillation). Given target size m', with d the log-space Euclidean metric:

G^{\text{per}} = \arg\max_{I \subseteq \{1,\dots,m\}, \, |I|=m'} \; \min_{\substack{i,j \in I \\ i \neq j}} d\big((f_i,b_i,\tau_i), (f_j,b_j,\tau_j)\big).

This is the max-min dispersion problem. The greedy farthest-first traversal provides a performance guarantee of two when distances satisfy the triangle inequality, and obtaining a guarantee of less than two is NP-hard. The 2-approximation is the best possible in general metric spaces unless P = NP. No (1-1/e) guarantee applies to the max-min objective; that guarantee belongs to a different problem class.

When distillation reduces the active filter count, the filter bank retains m channels, and inactive slots are set to zero in \Phi_G to preserve input dimension across all groups.

2.7 Transmission and variation

Multiplicative mutation:

\mathcal{V}_\sigma(G) = \big( (f_i e^{\eta_i}, b_i e^{\beta_i}, \tau_i e^{\gamma_i}) \big)_{i=1}^{m},
\qquad
\eta_i, \beta_i, \gamma_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2).

Offspring genome: G_{t+1} = \mathcal{V}_\sigma(\Pi(G_t)), where \Pi is the distillation operator and \sigma is fixed across conditions.

2.8 Organizational control P\text{-}S

Definition 6 (Organizational permutation). Given G = ((f_i,b_i,\tau_i))_{i=1}^m and a random permutation \pi:

G^{\text{P-S}} = \big( (f_{\pi(i)}, b_{\pi(i)}, \tau_{\pi(i)}) \big)_{i=1}^{m}.

Proposition 1. The multisets \{f_i\}, \{b_i\}, \{\tau_i\} are invariant under G \mapsto G^{\text{P-S}}; hence all marginal distributions are preserved exactly. A permutation reorders the index set, and multisets are invariant under reindexing. \square

Functional interpretation and non-triviality condition. The organization of G is the sequential assignment of triples to positions 1,\dots,m. This assignment may encode relational structure such as frequency progression or frequency–bandwidth coupling. G^{\text{P-S}} breaks this assignment while preserving all marginals.

Standard RNNs are order-sensitive by default; permutation invariance requires explicit regularization or architectural constraint. An i.i.d. random W_{zh} trained with Adam is exchangeable under channel permutation, so P\text{-}S is null by construction unless the controller has frequency-axis structure. Correction: The controller must be architecturally order-sensitive. We enforce this by replacing the flat input layer with a one-dimensional convolution along the channel axis:

z_t^{\text{conv}} = \operatorname{ReLU}(W_{\text{conv}} * z_t + b_{\text{conv}}),

where W_{\text{conv}} is a fixed random 1-D convolutional kernel of width w (e.g., w = 5). This ensures that adjacent channels are coupled and that channel order matters. The test of non-triviality is distributional: the learning-speed distribution of P\text{-}S must differ from that of P_{\text{Per}}. A single fixed-\theta output comparison is insufficient because it tests one instantiation, not the distribution induced by training.

