3. Architecture

3.1 Signal generator

The spectral composition of each environment is specified as follows:

\mathcal{E}_{\text{base}}:

· A: energy in [20,50] Hz, plus f_\star = 100 Hz.
· B: energy in [200,500] Hz, plus f_\star = 100 Hz.
· \varnothing: noise only.

\mathcal{E}_{\text{dist}}: same structure as \mathcal{E}_{\text{base}}, with shifted frequencies, amplitudes, and noise realizations.

\mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}}:

· A: energy in [20,50] Hz, without f_\star.
· B: energy in [200,500] Hz, without f_\star.
· C: f_\star = 100 Hz alone predicts C.

In \mathcal{E}_{\text{base}} and \mathcal{E}_{\text{dist}}, f_\star is present in both A and B and is non-discriminative. In \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}}, f_\star is the sole predictor of C. This resolves the inconsistency between the environment table and the signal parameters: A and B are not unchanged in \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}} with respect to f_\star; they lose it. The semantic shift is that the same physical feature (f_\star) acquires new predictive meaning (C rather than nothing).

3.2 Filter bank layer

· Gammatone filters of order n=4.
· m = 32 channels, log-spaced in [20,1000] Hz.
· b_i = 1.019 \, \operatorname{ERB}(f_i).
· \tau_i = k/f_i with k = 3.
· Envelope via leaky integrator.
· Inactive slots zeroed after distillation.

3.3 Controller

· Hidden dimension d_h = 64, input dimension m = 32, output dimension |\mathcal{Y}| = 4.
· Input layer: 1-D convolution along channel axis with fixed random kernel W_{\text{conv}} of width w = 5.
· Order sensitivity is architectural, not incidental.

3.4 Intergenerational loop with corrected data handling

1. Generation 0: initialize G_0, train \theta_0 on \mathcal{E}_{\text{base}}.
2. For t = 1,2,\dots,N_{\text{gen}}:
   · (a) Sample \mathcal{D}_{\text{sel}} from the parent’s environment \mathcal{E}_{t-1}, not the current environment \mathcal{E}_t. This eliminates the generational data leakage that would otherwise allow distillation to see post-shift data in the first generation of Phase 3.
   · (b) Compute \Delta_i for all i on \mathcal{D}_{\text{sel}} using \theta_{t-1}.
   · (c) G_t \leftarrow \Pi(G_{t-1}) according to group.
   · (d) G_t \leftarrow \mathcal{V}_\sigma(G_t).
   · (e) Reset \theta \leftarrow random init for all groups except L\text{-}\theta and L\text{-}G\theta.
   · (f) Train \theta_t on \mathcal{D}_{\text{train}} \sim \mathcal{E}_t with budget B.
   · (g) Evaluate \theta_t on \mathcal{D}_{\text{test}} \sim \mathcal{E}_t.
3. Report \{J_t\}.

Parental selection. Single-parent transmission: the best individual of the previous generation. Fixed across conditions.

