4. Experimental Design

4.1 Groups

Group Inherited perceptual structure Inherited controller Purpose
D None (random) No Baseline
L\text{-}\theta Fixed shared genome Yes Lamarckian controller inheritance only
L\text{-}G\theta Inherited genome Yes Lamarckian controller and genome inheritance
P_{\text{Sup}} Supervised distillation No Utility-based inheritance
P_{\text{Per}} Perceptual distillation No Diversity-based inheritance
P_R Random subset, size m' No Dimensionality-reduction control
P_S Permuted organization No Organizational-structure control

All groups use identical m, identical budgets B, identical controller architectures, and identical random seeds. Active filter capacity is equalized: group D uses m' = 16 active filters (not 32), matching the distillation target size of the P groups.

4.2 Environments and transitions

Phase Training environment Test environment
Phase 1 \mathcal{E}_{\text{base}} \mathcal{E}_{\text{base}}
Phase 2 \mathcal{E}_{\text{dist}} \mathcal{E}_{\text{dist}}
Phase 3 \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}} \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}}

Phase 3 is the semantic-shift test: the inherited f_\star filter becomes informative only here.

4.3 Metrics

Primary metric. N^\star_{85}: number of training episodes required to reach 85\% balanced accuracy on \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}}.

Secondary metrics.

· Pre-learning accuracy on \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}} (untrained controller).
· Generalization gap: |\text{acc}_{\mathcal{E}_{\text{base}}} - \text{acc}_{\mathcal{E}_{\text{dist}}}|.
· Prototype stability: variance of inherited f_i across generations.
· Confusion matrix on (A, B, C, \varnothing).

4.4 Oracle and anti-oracle brackets

To interpret the magnitude of any inheritance effect, include two additional conditions:

· Oracle: genome containing a single filter at f_\star = 100 Hz plus noise-matched filters.
· Anti-oracle: genome with all filters placed in the noise-only band, containing no filter near f_\star.

These bracket the best and worst possible perceptual starting points and contextualize the performance of P_{\text{Per}} and P_{\text{Sup}}.

4.5 Manipulation checks

1. Spectral separability: Verify that the ERB-overlap between the f_\star filter and all filters in [20,50] Hz is below \rho_{\max} = 0.3.
2. G^{\text{per}} retains f_\star: After Phase 1 perceptual distillation, confirm that at least one filter in G^{\text{per}} has center frequency within \pm 10\% of f_\star. If not, the diversity criterion is not preserving the semantically relevant filter, and the interpretation of P_{\text{Per}} is undermined.
3. Order sensitivity: Verify that the learning-speed distribution of P\text{-}S differs from that of P_{\text{Per}} via a Kolmogorov–Smirnov test on N^\star_{85} across seeds.

4.6 Falsification criteria

Favorable outcome. P_{\text{Per}} significantly reduces N^\star_{85} versus D, P_R, and P_S after correction, while matching D on \mathcal{E}_{\text{base}}.

Unfavorable outcome. P_{\text{Per}} is indistinguishable from D, P_R, or P_S; or its advantage disappears when m', B, and controller capacity are equalized.

Ambiguous outcome. P_{\text{Per}} accelerates learning on \mathcal{E}_{\text{dist}} but not on \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}}. This indicates local adaptation without reinterpretation.

Interpretation of P\text{-}S. If P\text{-}S \approx P_{\text{Per}}, organization is not functionally relevant; marginals suffice. If P\text{-}S < P_{\text{Per}}, organization matters.

Interpretation of P_{\text{Sup}} vs P_{\text{Per}}. In \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}}, P_{\text{Sup}} is expected to lose the f_\star filter during Phase 1 distillation because f_\star is not discriminative for A vs B. P_{\text{Per}} is expected to retain it. If P_{\text{Per}} outperforms P_{\text{Sup}} on Phase 3, this is direct evidence for the tension between present utility and future reusability.

