5. Statistical Analysis

5.1 Primary comparison and correction

Pre-registered primary comparison: P_{\text{Per}} vs D on N^\star_{85} in Phase 3. Five pairwise comparisons with Holm correction (not Bonferroni): Holm is uniformly more powerful and controls family-wise error without the unnecessary conservatism of Bonferroni. Corrected significance threshold for the primary comparison is \alpha = 0.05/5 = 0.01.

5.2 Power analysis

With n = 10 paired observations, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test at \alpha = 0.01 and r_{rb} = 0.5 has approximately 10\% power, not 80\%. To achieve 80\% power for r_{rb} = 0.5, approximately n \approx 55 seeds are required. To achieve 80\% power for r_{rb} = 0.9, n = 10 is sufficient. Pre-registered seed count: n = 55 per group per phase. If computational constraints require n = 10, the study is explicitly underpowered for moderate effects and must be reported as such.

5.3 Censoring of N^\star_{85}

N^\star_{85} is right-censored: some runs may not reach 85\% accuracy within the budget B. Treat N^\star_{85} as a censored survival time and use a log-rank test or a Cox proportional hazards model with group as the covariate. Report the censoring rate per group. If censoring exceeds 20\%, the primary analysis is the survival model, not the Wilcoxon test.

5.4 Effect size

Matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation r_{rb}. Interpretation: |r_{rb}| \approx 0.1 (small), \approx 0.3 (moderate), \ge 0.5 (large).

---

6. Limitations and Open Questions

1. Label availability. Both distillation operators assume labeled data. Unsupervised alternatives (predictive error, information gain) remain to be defined.
2. Order sensitivity. The P\text{-}S control is non-trivial only if the controller is architecturally order-sensitive. The 1-D convolutional input layer ensures this, but the distributional test in Section 4.5 must confirm it empirically.
3. Novelty status. No systematic review has been completed. The claim of novelty is provisional.
4. Semantic shift as a design choice. The specific shift (f_\star predicts C) is one of many. Results should be replicated with at least two alternative shifts.
5. Relation to existing receptive-field evolution. AISE-0.3 should be compared against a direct co-evolution baseline in which filters and controllers are evolved jointly, to verify that distillation adds value beyond direct evolution.
6. Baldwinian positioning. AISE transmits perceptual structure, not learning rules. The relation to Baldwinian meta-learning is one of complementary mechanisms, not competition.

---

7. Conclusion

AISE-0.3 corrects the mathematical ambiguities of the previous specification and separates two failure modes: present utility (supervised distillation) and future reusability (perceptual distillation). The organizational control P\text{-}S is well posed and preserves all marginal distributions. The experimental design eliminates generational data leakage, equalizes active filter capacity, enforces architectural order sensitivity, and pre-registers a primary comparison with a correctly powered statistical plan. The central scientific question remains:

Can a sensory representation selected during one generation retain features useful for a future task, even when the predictive meaning of those features is not preserved and the controller must be relearned?

AISE-0.3 is built to answer this question with a design that produces an informative result whether the hypothesis is confirmed or refuted.

Appendices

Appendix A — Mathematical Derivations

A.1 Gammatone Filter Bank: Bandwidth and Temporal Integration

The gammatone impulse response of order  n  is

h_{f,b}(t) = t^{\,n-1} e^{-2\pi b t} \cos(2\pi f t + \varphi),
\qquad t \ge 0.

The bandwidth parameter is related to the equivalent rectangular bandwidth (ERB) by

b = 1.019 \, \operatorname{ERB}(f),
\qquad
\operatorname{ERB}(f) = 24.7(0.00437 f + 1)
= 24.7 + 0.108 f,

where the factor  1.019  accounts for the difference between the gammatone bandwidth and the ERB scale. The temporal-integration constant is initialized to span several carrier periods:

\tau_i = \frac{k}{f_i},
\qquad k \ge 3.

This ensures that the integrator cutoff  1/\tau_i  is well below the carrier frequency  f_i , so that ripple at  2f_i  is attenuated by at least a factor of  k/(2\pi) . The ERB floors near  25  Hz, so filters in  [20,50]  Hz and a filter at  f_\star = 100  Hz overlap heavily. Spectral separability is verified explicitly in Appendix D.5.

A.2 Leaky Integrator Envelope Detector

The envelope  z_{i,t}  is computed by the first-order recursion

z_{i,t} = \alpha_i z_{i,t-1} + (1-\alpha_i) |u_{i,t}|,
\qquad
\alpha_i = \exp\!\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_i}\right),

with  \Delta t = 1/f_s  and  z_{i,0}=0 . This is a standard leaky integrator with unity DC gain. For a constant input  |u_{i,t}| = c , the steady-state response is

z_{i,\infty} = \alpha_i z_{i,\infty} + (1-\alpha_i) c
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
z_{i,\infty} = c.

The system is stable for  0 < \alpha_i < 1 , which holds for all  \tau_i > 0  and  \Delta t > 0 . The effective integration window is  \tau_{\text{eff}} = \tau_i .

A.3 Zero-Ablation: Dimension Preservation

For a trained controller  \theta^\star  with input dimension  m , the zero-ablation operator sets  z_{i,t} \leftarrow 0  for all  t  while leaving all other channels unchanged:

\Phi_G^{(i \to 0)} = (z_{1,t}, \dots, z_{i-1,t}, 0, z_{i+1,t}, \dots, z_{m,t})^\top.

The input dimension remains  m , so the controller’s weight matrices  W_{zh} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h \times m}  are unchanged. This isolates the contribution of channel  i  without requiring retraining.

A.4 Max-Min Dispersion: Greedy Farthest-First Traversal

The perceptual distillation problem is

G^{\text{per}} = \arg\max_{I \subseteq \{1,\dots,m\}, \, |I|=m'} \min_{\substack{i,j \in I \\ i \neq j}} d\big((f_i,b_i,\tau_i), (f_j,b_j,\tau_j)\big).

This is the max-min dispersion problem (remote-edge problem). The greedy farthest-first traversal proceeds as follows:

1. Start with an arbitrary point  i_0 \in \{1,\dots,m\} .
2. For  k = 2,\dots,m' , select

i_k = \arg\max_{i \notin \{i_0,\dots,i_{k-1}\}} \min_{j \in \{i_0,\dots,i_{k-1}\}} d\big((f_i,b_i,\tau_i), (f_j,b_j,\tau_j)\big).

Ravi, Rosenkrantz, and Tayi (1994) show that when distances satisfy the triangle inequality, this heuristic provides a performance guarantee of  2 , and obtaining a guarantee of less than  2  is NP-hard. The  2 -approximation is the best possible in general metric spaces unless  P = NP . No  (1-1/e)  guarantee applies to the max-min objective; that guarantee belongs to a different problem class (sum-min diversity with LP-based methods). The initialization in Algorithm 2 uses

i_0 = \arg\max_i \sum_{j \neq i} d\big((f_i,b_i,\tau_i), (f_j,b_j,\tau_j)\big),

which selects the most isolated point. The  2 -approximation guarantee holds for any initial point, so this initialization is valid, but it is outlier-seeking rather than densest-point-seeking.

