Appendix B — Proofs and Lemmas

B.1 Proof of Proposition 1 (Marginal Preservation Under Permutation)

Proposition. Let  G = ((f_i,b_i,\tau_i))_{i=1}^m  be an ordered genome and let  \pi  be a permutation of  \{1,\dots,m\} . Define

G^{\text{P-S}} = ((f_{\pi(i)}, b_{\pi(i)}, \tau_{\pi(i)}))_{i=1}^m.

Then the multisets  \{f_i\}_{i=1}^m ,  \{b_i\}_{i=1}^m , and  \{\tau_i\}_{i=1}^m  are invariant under  G \mapsto G^{\text{P-S}} . Consequently, the marginal distributions of  f ,  b , and  \tau  are preserved exactly.

Proof. A multiset is a pair  (S, \mu)  where  S  is a set and  \mu : S \to \mathbb{N}_{>0}  is a multiplicity function. The permutation  \pi  acts on the index set  \{1,\dots,m\}  by reindexing. For any value  v \in \mathbb{R}_{>0} ,

\mu_{G^{\text{P-S}}}(v) = |\{ i : f_{\pi(i)} = v \}| = |\{ \pi(i) : f_{\pi(i)} = v \}| = |\{ j : f_j = v \}| = \mu_G(v),

where the second equality follows because  \pi  is a bijection. The same argument applies to  b  and  \tau . Hence the multisets are invariant, and therefore all marginal distributions are preserved.  \square 

B.2 Lemma: Order Sensitivity of Recurrent Controllers with 1-D Convolution

Lemma. A recurrent network of the form

z_t^{\text{conv}} = \operatorname{ReLU}(W_{\text{conv}} * z_t + b_{\text{conv}}),
\qquad
h_t = \tanh(W_{hh} h_{t-1} + W_{zh} z_t^{\text{conv}} + b_h),

where  W_{\text{conv}}  is a fixed random 1-D convolutional kernel of width  w \ge 3 , is order-sensitive with respect to the input channels  z_{1,t},\dots,z_{m,t} .

Proof. A 1-D convolution with a non-constant kernel  W_{\text{conv}}  does not commute with arbitrary permutations of the input channels. For a permutation matrix  P  that is not a circular shift,  W_{\text{conv}} * (P z) \neq P (W_{\text{conv}} * z)  in general. Consequently, the hidden-state update depends on the order of the input channels, and the outputs  \hat{y}_t  differ. Therefore the network is order-sensitive.  \square 

Remark. An i.i.d. random  W_{zh}  trained with Adam is exchangeable under channel permutation, so the organizational control  P\text{-}S  would be null by construction without the convolutional input layer. The 1-D convolution is the architectural feature that makes  P\text{-}S  non-trivial.

B.3 Lemma: Stability of the Multiplicative Mutation Operator

Lemma. Let  G = ((f_i,b_i,\tau_i))_{i=1}^m  and let  \mathcal{V}_\sigma(G) = ((f_i e^{\eta_i}, b_i e^{\beta_i}, \tau_i e^{\gamma_i}))_{i=1}^m  with  \eta_i,\beta_i,\gamma_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2) . Then the expected log-parameters are preserved:

\mathbb{E}[\log f_i'] = \log f_i,
\qquad
\mathbb{E}[\log b_i'] = \log b_i,
\qquad
\mathbb{E}[\log \tau_i'] = \log \tau_i.

Proof.  \log f_i' = \log f_i + \eta_i , and  \mathbb{E}[\eta_i] = 0 . The same holds for  b_i  and  \tau_i .  \square 

