Appendix D — Experimental Protocols

D.1 Group Definitions

Group Inherited perceptual structure Inherited controller
 D  None (random genome) No
 L\text{-}\theta  Fixed shared genome Yes
 L\text{-}G\theta  Inherited genome Yes
 P_{\text{Sup}}  Supervised distillation No
 P_{\text{Per}}  Perceptual distillation No
 P_R  Random subset of size  m'  No
 P_S  Permuted organization No

All groups use identical  m , identical budgets  B , identical controller architectures, and identical random seeds. Active filter capacity is equalized: group  D  uses  m' = 16  active filters, matching the distillation target size of the  P  groups.

D.2 Phase Structure

Phase 1 — Base environment. All groups train on  \mathcal{E}_{\text{base}} . Perceptual-inheritance groups distill and transmit  G . Lamarckian groups transmit  \theta .

Phase 2 — Distribution shift. All groups continue from Phase 1. Training and testing on  \mathcal{E}_{\text{dist}} .

Phase 3 — Semantic shift. All groups continue from Phase 2. Training and testing on  \mathcal{E}_{\text{sem}} . The  f_\star  filter becomes informative only here.

D.3 Generation Loop (Detailed)

For  t = 1,\dots,N_{\text{gen}} :

1. Sample  \mathcal{D}_{\text{sel}} \sim \mathcal{E}_{t-1}  (parent’s environment).
2. Compute  \Delta_i^{\text{zero}}  for all  i  on  \mathcal{D}_{\text{sel}}  using  \theta_{t-1} .
3. Distill:  G_t \leftarrow \Pi(G_{t-1})  according to group.
4. Mutate:  G_t \leftarrow \mathcal{V}_\sigma(G_t) .
5. Reset  \theta : random init for all groups except  L\text{-}\theta  and  L\text{-}G\theta .
6. Train  \theta_t  on  \mathcal{D}_{\text{train}} \sim \mathcal{E}_t  with budget  B .
7. Evaluate  \theta_t  on  \mathcal{D}_{\text{test}} \sim \mathcal{E}_t .
8. Record  J_t .

D.4 Oracle and Anti-Oracle Brackets

· Oracle: genome containing a single filter at  f_\star = 100  Hz plus noise-matched filters.
· Anti-oracle: genome with all filters placed in the noise-only band, containing no filter near  f_\star .

These bracket the best and worst possible perceptual starting points.

D.5 Manipulation Checks

1. Spectral separability: Verify that the ERB-overlap between the  f_\star  filter and all filters in  [20,50]  Hz is below  \rho_{\max} = 0.3 . If not, adjust  f_\star  or  m .
2.  G^{\text{per}}  retains  f_\star : After Phase 1 perceptual distillation, confirm that at least one filter in  G^{\text{per}}  has center frequency within  \pm 10\%  of  f_\star . If not, the diversity criterion is not preserving the semantically relevant filter.
3. Order sensitivity: Verify that the learning-speed distribution of  P\text{-}S  differs from that of  P_{\text{Per}}  via a Kolmogorov–Smirnov test on  N^\star_{85}  across seeds.

D.6 Statistical Plan

· Primary comparison:  P_{\text{Per}}  vs  D  on  N^\star_{85}  in Phase 3.
· Correction: Holm correction (not Bonferroni) for five pairwise comparisons.
· Power analysis: With  n = 10  paired observations, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test at  \alpha = 0.01  and  r_{rb} = 0.5  has approximately  10\%  power, not  80\% . To achieve  80\%  power for  r_{rb} = 0.5 , approximately  n \approx 55  seeds are required. Pre-registered seed count:  n = 55  per group per phase. If computational constraints require  n = 10 , the study is explicitly underpowered for moderate effects.
· Censoring:  N^\star_{85}  is right-censored. Use a log-rank test or Cox proportional hazards model. Report censoring rate per group. If censoring exceeds  20\% , the primary analysis is the survival model.
· Effect size: Matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation  r_{rb} .

